<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Visegrád Group</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1991</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Visegrád Four</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/visegrad">Visegrád</a> Group is a regional alliance of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czech-republic">Czechia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovakia">Slovakia</a>, founded at Visegrád in Hungary in February 1991 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia</a>, Hungary and Poland. Its name recalls the meeting of the kings of Hungary, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a> and Poland at Visegrád in 1335. It helped coordinate its members' path into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nato">NATO</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-union">European Union</a> and continues to promote cooperation in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/visegrad-group.jpg" alt="Visegrád Group" width="320" height="249" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CrazyPhunk · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Visegrad_group_countries.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
